    "reference": "Spenceley & Rylance (2021). Ecotourism and the sustainable development goals. Adapted text in DEC10 course materials (Week 4 student booklet, Reading to write 2B).",
    "citeAs": "Cite as Spenceley and Rylance (2021) or (Spenceley & Rylance, 2021).",
    "paragraphs": [
      "## Introduction",
      "A. In 2015, governments adopted the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). The agenda established a global framework to end extreme poverty, fight inequality and injustice, and remedy climate change. Building on the Millennium Development Goals (MDGs), 17 Sustainable Development Goals and 169 associated targets were agreed. Tourism has been included within the targets for Goal 8 on decent work and economic growth, Goal 12 on responsible consumption and production, and Goal 14 on life below water. However, tourism has the potential to contribute, directly or indirectly, to all of the goals (UNWTO, 2015). The following two sections present 2 of the SDGs and how they relate to tourism.",
      "## Goal 2: Zero hunger: End hunger, achieve food security and improved nutrition, and promote sustainable agriculture",
      "B. Tourism can help sustainable agriculture by promoting the production of local foods for hotels and restaurants and by increasing the sales of local products to tourists. For instance, agrotourism can provide extra income for farmers while offering tourists educational and enriching experiences (UNWTO & UNDP, 2017). Farming and using natural resources are still the main sources of jobs in many rural areas, especially in poor communities in developing countries, where they provide 55% of the employment and are the primary source of income (Schiere & Kater, 2001). A study of 49 tropical protected areas showed that they are becoming isolated as deforestation continues around their borders. This indicates that effective management should also focus on broader local economic and social issues (Naughton-Treves, Holland, & Brandon, 2005). It’s important for poor communities to have different ways to earn money. This helps them not rely only on one source of income, like farming, and reduces their risks of poverty (Ashley, Mdoe, & Reynolds, 2002).",
      "C. Marine-protected areas used by tourists and fishers help reduce hunger in several ways. For example, small community-managed marine protected areas can provide safe spaces where fish can breed and grow. This supports local fishing which many communities rely on for food. These areas also allow for tourism activities like whale shark viewing and manta ray diving, which create jobs for local people. A luxury ecotourism company, “andBeyond,” is working with local communities and authorities in Tanzania and Mozambique to develop such initiatives (Braack & Mearns, 2017). In these areas, there are also efforts to stop overfishing and the killing of endangered marine species, to protect reefs, and to teach local communities how to manage their fishing resources better and fish responsibly (Braack & Mearns, 2017).",
      "## Goal 3: Good health and wellbeing: Ensure healthy lives and promote wellbeing for all at all ages",
      "D. There is growing scientific evidence that protected areas have health benefits. The theme “Healthy Parks Healthy People” was a main focus at the IUCN 2014 World Parks Congress in Sydney, Australia (Spenceley, 2017). Visiting places with lots of biodiversity can prevent health issues like obesity, heart disease, depression, and anxiety (Sparkes & Woods, 2009). In Australia, Parks Victoria has teamed up with Medibank Australia and the National Heart Foundation. They allow health care professionals to suggest physical activities in protected areas as a way to prevent diseases (2017). With the COVID-19 pandemic, visiting natural areas became even more important to help ease the stress of lockdowns (Spenceley, 2021; Spenceley et al., 2021).",
      "E. Promoting sports tourism is gaining interest in protected areas as it helps generate revenue for conservation and shows the positive impact of biodiversity on local communities. A great example of this is seen in the adventure tourism events in the southern African transfrontier conservation areas. These include the Tour de Tuli, a cross-border mountain biking event, Desert Knights, which combines canoeing and mountain biking, and Wildruns, which are cross-border trail runs (Spenceley, 2018a). These sports events not only help in funding conservation efforts but also contribute to local economies by creating job opportunities and showcasing the social benefits of preserving biodiversity.",
      "F. Corporate social responsibility efforts by ecotourism companies in areas rich in biodiversity often lead to improvements in public health. Many tourism businesses use their CSR funds to help develop rural clinics and hospitals or to provide essential medical equipment. A good example is the Africa Foundation, which directs donations from guests and donors of andBeyond to community projects near their lodges, like the Phinda Game Reserve in South Africa. For over twenty years, these contributions have supported health initiatives such as building a clinic in Mduku, refurbishing another clinic, conducting HIV/AIDS awareness workshops, enhancing water supply, and starting a school permaculture project (Snyman & Spenceley, 2019)."
    ]
