    "paragraphs": [
      "A. The notion of ecotourism was introduced in the late 1970s. At that time, it was intended to be ecologically responsible, promote conservation, benefit local populations and help travellers connect with biocultural diversity. Now it is more a marketing term. It is used to give adventure-tourism packages a more “responsible” sheen. However, it is not always as responsible as it seems. For example, visitors might get a nature walk, but interactions with local residents are limited to souvenir sellers. In the meantime, international consortiums arrange everything and keep the profits for themselves. While it is no surprise that the original concept of ecotourism has been hijacked, it becomes more problematic when it blocks local communities from ancestral lands or even involves their forced relocation. As an illustration of this, 16 villages on Rempang Island, Indonesia were recently evicted to build an eco-city with a solar panel factory. While the need to increase renewable energy production is urgent, it is hard to justify when it happens at the expense of local residents’ lives and territorial sovereignty.",
      "B. Outcomes such as this have been occurring due to questionable actions by multinationals. They have been able to hide under the cover of unclear terms such as sustainable or regenerative development. This is a practice referred to as bluewashing. Bluewashing allows corporations to pursue their own interests rather than achieving genuine ecotourism goals. They imply that their work is virtuous, despite the reality. To explore this issue, in June 2023 a group of researchers at Grenoble Ecole de Management (GEM) organised a dialogue with members of the Mbyá Guaraní community from Maricá, Brazil. The purpose was to explore the behaviour of multinational corporations toward indigenous peoples and their land rights. This article gives details of the dialogue.",
      "C. In Maricá, residents of the Mbyá Guaraní village of Ka’Aguy Hovy Porã are now facing the possibility of being pushed aside for a massive resort branded as Maraey. The firm leading the project, IDB do Brasil, asserts that it has the required legal permits to proceed with the resort. However, in countries such as this, building permits are unclear and not very strict. It should also be noted that corruption has been a frequent problem. Legal conflicts often happen between local governments, state governments, and national courts. Sometimes, these conflicts also create divisions within indigenous families. When asked to provide specific information on the company’s interactions with the community, Maraey’s CEO, Emilio Izquierdo, explained that an agreement was signed in December 2021 between the company and the indigenous community’s cacique or main representative. Izquierdo said that, as part of the agreement, the local government promised to find a public area where the community could stay permanently. Maraey representatives stated that such an area was purchased in December 2022 but declined to provide additional information about the purchase.",
      "D. Tupã Nunes, leader of the Mbyá Guaraní community, pointed out that he had no knowledge of the 2022 agreement signed with the chief. Furthermore, the chief does not appear to have shared any news of it with her community. According to the Guarani tradition of governance, sharing information is a crucial obligation of the cacique. Obscure practices like this have fostered deep fractures within the community itself. Members only discovered the extent of the local government’s involvement and the advanced state of the project when the bulldozers arrived to clear the land. It should be noted that the International Labor Organization’s C169 agreement on Indigenous and Tribal Peoples, signed by both Spain and Brazil, requires at least a dialogue with indigenous communities before launching projects that would affect them.",
      "E. The discovery of a number of irregularities as well as conflict between the community and the construction workers in April 2023 led local courts to suspend the project. A Superior Court of Justice document listed reasons for the suspension. These included constant pressures on the water system and the illegal environmental licensing process. Maraey representatives have asserted that all licenses were obtained after a strict process with the State Environmental Institute (INEA). IDB do Brazil maintains that the 54-hectare project will be sustainable and inclusive. It will also contain the promised facilities, including a hospital and schools. However, there will also be mall and an 18-hole golf course, and 150,000 to 300,000 tourists are projected to visit annually. Given that the project is also forecasted to generate the equivalent of 197 million US dollars, there is a lot more than environmental and social concerns at stake.",
      "F. The significance of the Amazon rainforest and Atlantic coastal forest for indigenous peoples such as the Guarani Mbyá goes far beyond a simple habitat. They derive their culture, language and social order from the natural structure of the forest. In response to this, the International Financial Reporting Standards Foundation has recently asked for more scrutiny on issues that are not related to climate, especially social and societal problems. For multinationals, however, the temptation will always be there to find ways to minimise risks and continue business as usual.",
      "G. Research has shown that lax reporting and the lack of enforcement mechanisms have led firms to shirk social sustainability and human rights requirements. Firms have instead favoured bluewashing strategies. This has enabled multinationals to increasingly follow what historian Patrick Wolfe called a “logic of elimination” that erases natives from the land. However, there is reason to think that attitudes can shift over time. A 2019 victory in Bahía of the Tupinamba de Olivença tribe over the Portuguese hotel giant Vila Gale created a legal precedent. This demonstrated that if local authorities license projects without involving federal agencies, it can backfire. For Juliana Batista, human rights lawyer for the Brazilian NGO Instituto Socio-Ambiental involved in the case, it is a matter of understanding the nature of indigenous land rights which for her “take precedence over any other rights.”"
    ]
