    "note": "Question: What is the key challenge in transitioning to a “green” job market? The small numbers (¹ ² ³ …) show the sentence numbers.",
    "paragraphs": [
      "¹The increasing necessity to adopt more environmentally sustainable business practices has led to the emergence of notions of a “green economy” and “green jobs.” ²While the creation or classification of such jobs may be a positive step forward in meeting sustainability goals, the transition towards a green job market is marked by significant challenges, including the absence of a clear definition of green jobs which impedes effective policy implementation. ³However, the most significant barrier to achieving a smooth transition will undoubtedly be the readiness of the workforce to adapt to this new market given the noticeable skills gap, which has the potential to hinder progress.",
      "¹The ambiguity in defining what constitutes a “green job” will likely pose somewhat of a challenge, at least initially. ²As outlined by Bowen et al. (2018) and expanded upon by Vidican Auktor (2020), green jobs vary widely in their nature and scope, resulting in difficulties developing a uniform definition. ³This leads to classification inconsistencies and complications measuring green growth's labour market impact, hindering effective workforce planning and policy implementation (Vidican Auktor, 2020). ⁴However, focusing on specific skill requirements for green jobs may be helpful here as these skills provide more detailed insights into the necessary changes for transitioning to a green economy, including training needs (Vidican Auktor, 2020). ⁵In fact, the development of a Green General Skill (GGS) index has assisted in classifying and contextualising green jobs within a broader skills framework (Vidican Auktor, 2020), making it easier to understand and strategize for green job training and growth.",
      "¹Therefore, a greater challenge is likely to be around the readiness of the workforce to adapt to green jobs. ²According to LinkedIn's global green skills report, there is a burgeoning demand for green skills that is not being met by the current rate of skill development (Hyman & Smith, 2023). ³As Sue Duke, Vice President of Global Public Policy & Economic Graph at LinkedIn, reports, only one in every eight workers have the necessary green skills (Hyman & Smith, 2023). ⁴She adds that this skills gap is widening as environmental sustainability requirements rapidly evolve across all industries. ⁵Hence, the transition demands not only new skills but also a substantial re-skilling of the existing workforce. ⁶However, while researchers using the O*NET database have established that green occupations tend to involve high-level analytical and specialized skills (Vidican Auktor, 2020; Bowen et al., 2018), there is still not enough research into how workforce capabilities match the skills required for a green job market (Bowen et al., 2018). ⁷Without this information, it is extremely difficult to construct a measure of skill distance and therefore the extent of training or retraining required for a successful transition to a green job market.",
      "¹In conclusion, while developing a standardized definition of green jobs and skills is a challenge for policy-makers, there are tools available to assist. ²Therefore, it is the preparedness of the workforce that will likely present the greatest challenge in the transition to a green job market. ³The skills gap may impede a smooth transition as workers struggle to adapt to the evolving job market, so comprehensive research for planning the reskilling of the workforce will be critical."
    ]
