      "¹International tourism has grown into a vast global industry, bringing with it an intense focus on its environmental and social impacts. ²In response to the negative impacts, ecotourism has emerged as a form of responsible tourism (Alom, 2023; Mielly, 2023; Spenceley & Rylance, 2021). ³At the same time, as the concept of ecotourism has evolved, so have the complexities and challenges associated with the implementation of its goals. ⁴Despite this, however, a number of goals, such as supporting livelihoods and preserving the environment, do remain achievable at the local level, even though those at the global level are an ongoing challenge.",
      "¹The goals of ecotourism align with a number of Sustainable Development Goals to improve the lives of local communities (Spenceley & Rylance, 2021). ² Costa Rica is a successful model of how ecotourism has achieved this by supporting the local economy by involving stakeholders at the local and small-scale level, which, in turn, has contributed to the GDP and stability of Costa Rica overall (Alom, 2023). ³Projects in Africa similarly support the local workforce (Spenceley & Rylance, 2021). ⁴Admittedly, as Mielly (2023) points out, successes such as these are countered by less desirable practices, such as bluewashing, in apparent attempts to support ecotourism. ⁵However, as Mielly also points out, attitudes can change. ⁶This means, therefore, that by simply highlighting improper behaviour of tourism operators is likely to pressure them into ensuring their activities achieve the goals of ecotourism.",
      "¹The potential for ecotourism to achieve its goals goes beyond benefitting local communities to also promoting environmental conservation. ²Again, as Spenceley and Rylance (2021) explain, the Sustainable Development Goals align with those of ecotourism by focusing on environmental preservation through programs such as marine protection and agrotourism. ³Indeed, the writers add, positive outcomes in this regard can be seen occurring at the local level where environmental preservation is occurring, including sports tourism and corporate social responsibility projects. ⁴Unfortunately, there are also examples of environmental destruction as a consequence of tourism developments (Mielly, 2023). ⁵Nonetheless, such destruction, regrettable as it is, is clearly not preventing otherwise positive outcomes from being achieved.",
      "¹At the same time, it can be argued that any gains achieved at the local level will be negated by ongoing environmental impact of tourism at the global level. ²Alom (2023) makes the valid point that, taking into account the necessary aspects of travel such as accommodation and air transport, tourism overall contributes 10% to global CO2 emissions. ³The writer suggests addressing this problem by investing in new technology and new fuels, and by tourists making changes to the frequency and method of travel. ⁴These clearly constitute more long-term approaches towards achieving the goals of ecotourism at the global level, but this does not mean that local achievements are outweighed.",
      "¹Ecotourism, while based on important local and global goals, is more likely to achieve the former than the latter, at least in the short term. ²Whereas the global goal of reducing the impact on climate change will require time, the goals of improving the lives and the environments of local populations are already seeing tangible results. ³It is important that there is ongoing awareness in order to maximise the contribution ecotourism is already making, and has the potential to continue making, to achieving its important social and environmental goals."
